{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute $\\mathbf f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}4\\right),$ we substitute $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}4$ into the expression for $\\mathbf f'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198230 #edb/ref 1069 "e-609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198230 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 10t^4 ,\\, 6\\ln t\\rangle,$ what is $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c737d317-8e09-5ccb-b816-b5f56e925ba9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198230 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t) = 10t^4,$ we have\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 40t^3,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nx'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = 40\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^3 = 5.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t) = 6 \\ln t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6}{t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{6}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)}=12.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨{x}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,\\,12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198231 #edb/ref 1069 "e-611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198231 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t^6,\\, t^5\\rangle,$ Calculate $\\mathbf r''(t).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cde8f304-4937-5eb7-a8e1-a08355ee4d4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198231 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t) = t^6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{5} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =30{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t) = t^5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5{t}^{4} \\\\ {y}^{″}(t) & =20{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨{x}^{″}(t),\\,{y}^{″}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨30{t}^{4},\\,20{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =10⟨3{t}^{4},\\,2{t}^{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186319368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198355 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198356 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198356 #edb/re